#edb/ref 17592186385970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162227 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "33210971-70cd-50fd-974b-053eab8e1cab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162858 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162858 #edb/ref 1061 648291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162858 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ede58be-b8d2-5bb7-a17c-e74da723f67c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162858 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162945 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162945 #edb/ref 1061 648292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162945 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93a722c1-3bdf-59db-908f-c5d0b209511b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162945 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162946 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162946 #edb/ref 1061 648298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162946 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a223abd-811a-561b-aba4-561597ec202e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162946 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162947 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186385970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162947 #edb/ref 1061 648295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162947 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1b7070be-3dac-5359-9175-8300793c3568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162947 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162948 #edb/ref 1061 648304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162948 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e87c19a4-7e24-5962-880c-39d60fa71758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162948 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162951 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162951 #edb/ref 1061 648301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162951 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cf890cb-057f-5300-9a99-426e3fb959db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162951 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163233 #edb/ref 1064 "We can compute a singular value decomposition (SVD) of any matrix! The algorithm for computing an SVD of a rectangular $m\\times n$ matrix $(m\\neq n)$ is similar to the algorithm for square matrices.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/f6/f65e0eee905da85ce0be4bbf39546c2b31c2bac082ff00a6a1d54fd5b89a4a4e.svg)\n\nNotice that $A$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix. So, to compute the SVD of $A,$ we must write\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\Sigma$ is a ${\\color{blue}{3}}\\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix with the singular values of $A$ (ordered from largest to smallest) on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{blue}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{3}}$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal ${\\color{red}{2}} \\times {\\color{red}{2}}$ matrix.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.**  Find the singular values of $A.$ Computing the eigenvalues of $A^TA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 4, \\qquad \\lambda_2 = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1} =  2,  \\qquad \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2} =  \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T A$). Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_1 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ and $(A^T \\! A - \\lambda_2 I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/07/07085f021218ff17060b3c1a9099e1cabad24b3d12269f97f1fe50d49b970f6b.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A.$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the first two columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $U$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix. However, we do not have the third singular value, so we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n![](images/71/71f89dbcee33bce284b3085b872fdb9da2cc41166fe8a0bd26ef0bb5fe6876aa.svg) \nsuch that \n![](images/9e/9edc744372650e86c27f31ef15ac575cc8203803bdc5fe5730390727fd37146a.svg)\nSolving this system, we get the following nonzero normalized solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c2/c2bbd771d886da6b3a369b7e03345d1be75c18c26c66590598581d2d99590524.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163233 #edb/ref 1065 4893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163233 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "670f2f0c-5dc5-54ae-9a39-2166ee2414fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163233 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163258 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find an SVD for the $2\\times 4$ matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/32/325fa7080fdd5f7fc5723a2304aac049a938dfd1b490f09a7a2ceabb0b934db8.svg)\n\nDirectly applying the standard approach to $A$ could be problematic because it requires finding the eigenvalues of the $4\\times 4$ matrix $A^T \\! A.$ \n\nIn such cases, it's usually more convenient to find an SVD of the transposed matrix $A^T$ and then take the transpose of the resulting SVD. In this case, we would only have to find the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nA^T = U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis an SVD for $A^T,$ then\n\n$$\nA= \\left(A^T\\right)^T= \\left(U \\Sigma V^T\\right)^T=V\\Sigma^T U^T\n$$\n\nis an SVD for $A.$  \n\nGoing through our usual process, we find that an SVD for $A^T$ is\n\n![](images/78/7802bcf50374a71efc4c0a7754c5628e88e7f1dfac345bc9e590cfcebd89d8d1.svg)\n\nTaking the transpose of the above equation. we find that an SVD for $A$ is\n\n![](images/77/77f464a1ce372dcd4e9aecb99b23342dee26fd5cf3f701ffa2fc858ebb4865b5.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163258 #edb/ref 1065 5110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163258 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "71ba93f6-01a1-54c4-bda4-b51b9b8dad77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163258 #edb/ref 1067 "The Singular Value Decomposition of a Transposed Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186371426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371423 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "89ed90b3-a22c-5487-9e5f-dfdcd163748b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371424 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371424 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371424 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "65acaa42-33cd-5757-90f9-4d51036d283f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371425 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371425 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371425 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c9e77905-5ec8-56d5-8779-68fa5b781883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371426 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "97daab37-f8ae-5701-b85b-b2e30894a4f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371427 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b4e0b9ef-caf7-5749-a8a6-77538e234c6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186371428 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5fd5a22-26d6-55d8-a7db-98cfc7d4ab6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-363830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ \\,\\,-1 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n|  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d47baabf-d20b-51dc-ba6e-5b8ddf2ee3e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381942 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381943 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381947 #edb/ref 17592186045